  "raw_meta_yaml": "context:\n  version: \"0.26.3\"\n\npackage:\n  name: all-smi\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/lablup/all-smi/archive/refs/tags/v${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 1feeb5f4d10489858220ce051b5685338063f0ad0b2588d8375bbc51516c62d8\n\nbuild:\n  number: 1\n  script:\n    content:\n      # cc-rs appends the generic ${CFLAGS} to every target it compiles for, so the\n      # build scripts it compiles for the build platform also get the host target's\n      # -march/-mabi flags and fail. The rust activation already mirrors these into\n      # the per-triple CFLAGS_<triple>/CXXFLAGS_<triple>/CPPFLAGS_<triple> variables,\n      # so the generic ones are redundant.\n      - if: linux and build_platform != target_platform\n        then:\n          - unset CFLAGS CXXFLAGS CPPFLAGS\n      # rustc links this target through `-fuse-ld=lld`, and rust-lld doesn't understand\n      # the ppc64le relocations GCC emits into zstd-sys' objects (\"unknown relocation\n      # (31) against symbol ERR_getErrorString\"). GCC honours the last -fuse-ld, so\n      # appending this switches us back to GNU ld.\n      - if: target_platform == \"linux-ppc64le\"\n        then:\n          - export CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS=\"${CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS:-} -C link-arg=-fuse-ld=bfd\"\n      # AWS-LC's ARM64 assembly is GAS syntax, which cl.exe doesn't understand — it\n      # skips the `.S` files with D9024/D9027 and the link then dies on the missing\n      # object (LNK1181, chacha-armv8.o). aws-lc-sys reaches for clang-cl on this\n      # target by itself, but only when no CC is set, and the compiler activation\n      # sets CC_aarch64_pc_windows_msvc=cl.exe. Its own AWS_LC_SYS_CC knob wins over\n      # that, and only for this crate: every other build script keeps using cl.exe.\n      - if: target_platform == \"win-arm64\"\n        then:\n          - set \"AWS_LC_SYS_CC=clang-cl\"\n      - if: unix\n        then:\n          - cargo auditable install --locked --no-track --bin all-smi --root ${{ PREFIX }} --path .\n        else:\n          - cargo auditable install --locked --no-track --bin all-smi --root %LIBRARY_PREFIX% --path .\n      - cargo-bundle-licenses --format yaml --output ./THIRDPARTY.yml\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('rust') }}\n    - cargo-bundle-licenses\n    - cargo-auditable\n    # aws-lc-sys, pulled in via rustls/russh, builds its C sources with cmake\n    - cmake\n    - if: unix\n      then: make\n    # aws-lc-sys assembles its x86 assembly with nasm, and needs clang-cl for the\n    # GAS-syntax ARM64 assembly (see the build script)\n    - if: target_platform == \"win-64\"\n      then: nasm\n    - if: target_platform == \"win-arm64\"\n      then: clang\n    # tonic-prost-build compiles the TPU gRPC client, Linux targets only\n    - if: linux\n      then: libprotobuf\n\ntests:\n  - script:\n      - all-smi --help\n      - all-smi --version\n  - package_contents:\n      bin:\n        - all-smi\n      strict: true\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://github.com/lablup/all-smi\n  summary: Command-line utility for monitoring GPU hardware\n  description: |\n    all-smi is a command-line utility for monitoring GPU and NPU hardware\n    across multiple systems. It provides a real-time view of accelerator\n    utilization, memory usage, temperature, power consumption, and other\n    metrics, and is designed to be a cross-platform alternative to\n    nvidia-smi with support for NVIDIA GPUs, AMD GPUs, NVIDIA Jetson\n    platforms, Apple Silicon GPUs, Intel GPUs, Intel Gaudi NPUs, Google\n    Cloud TPUs, Tenstorrent NPUs, Rebellions NPUs and Furiosa NPUs.\n\n    The application presents a terminal-based user interface with cluster\n    overview and interactive sorting, for both local and remote monitoring,\n    and can also export Prometheus metrics via its API mode.\n  license: Apache-2.0\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE\n    - THIRDPARTY.yml\n  repository: https://github.com/lablup/all-smi\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - pavelzw\n",
